      <h1>Sigil 2.8.5 and 2.8.6: CSS Parser, AVIF/JXL Images, OPF Fix</h1>
      <p class="meta"><time datetime="2026-11-12">Published November 12, 2026</time></p>

      <p>The open-source EPUB editor <a href="https://sigil-ebook.com/sigil">Sigil</a> shipped twice in one week. Version 2.8.5 arrived on November 5, 2026 with a ground-up replacement of its aging CSS engine, first-class AVIF and JPEG XL image support, and a batch of EPUB 3.4 spec-alignment changes. Four days later, version 2.8.6 landed as a pure bugfix release — and if you produce EPUB 3 files, it is the more important of the two. Sigil 2.8.5 contains a bug that can corrupt your book's OPF package document when you swap the cover image on an EPUB 3. The Sigil team recommends every 2.8.5 user update immediately.</p>

      <p>This post walks through what actually changed, why the CSS parser swap matters for day-to-day editing, what AVIF/JXL support means for next-generation covers, and how to check whether any of your existing EPUBs were hit by the cover-image bug.</p>

      <h2>Why Sigil still matters in 2026</h2>

      <p>Sigil remains the most direct way to edit EPUB files at the code level. Where <a href="/blog/calibre-vs-sigil-2026/">Calibre is a library manager that happens to convert</a>, Sigil is a dedicated EPUB editor: it shows you the OPF spine, the NCX/nav table of contents, and every XHTML and CSS resource inside the package, and it lets you edit them directly. If you have ever needed to repair a broken EPUB, strip out publisher cruft before sideloading, or fine-tune CSS for a specific e-reader, Sigil is the tool. Our earlier <a href="/blog/sigil-2-8-image-editing-epub-guide-2026/">Sigil 2.8 image-editing guide</a> covered the image workflow; this release changes some of the machinery underneath it.</p>

      <h2>Sigil 2.8.5: the CSS parser rewrite</h2>

      <p>The headline change in 2.8.5 is invisible: Sigil removed its outdated CSS parser and replaced it with a modern, spec-compliant parser built on <a href="https://github.com/lexbor/lexbor">Lexbor</a>, an open-source HTML/CSS rendering engine. For years, Sigil's CSS handling lagged behind the EPUB 3 spec — it would mis-parse newer selectors, drop or mangle at-rules it didn't recognize, and occasionally report phantom errors on perfectly valid stylesheets produced by other tools.</p>

      <p>In practice, three things improve:</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Validation is trustworthy again.</strong> The Reports → Validate CSS feature now understands modern CSS, so errors it flags are errors you actually need to fix before publishing.</li>
        <li><strong>Round-tripping is safer.</strong> EPUBs produced by web-first pipelines (Pandoc, static-site generators, in-browser converters) tend to ship newer CSS. Older Sigil versions could silently rewrite these rules into broken equivalents. The Lexbor parser preserves them.</li>
        <li><strong>Code view and Book Browser stay in sync.</strong> The Book Browser's bulk add/remove operations were also optimized in this release (contributed by rinne1998), so large-image imports no longer trigger a full-tree refresh per file.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>One caveat from the release notes: Sigil statically links its own patched internal copy of Lexbor, because required upstream changes are approved but not yet merged. Do not swap in a stock Lexbor build — the ABI differs and the editor will break.</p>

      <h2>AVIF and JPEG XL: next-generation cover images</h2>

      <p>Sigil 2.8.5 adds support for AVIF and JXL images through Qt's image-format plugin system, plus a clever preview trick: since QtWebEngine can't render JXL natively yet, Sigil polyfills JXL previews on the fly by converting to JPEG inside the Preview pane. That means you can drop a next-generation-format cover into your EPUB, see it correctly in Sigil's preview, and export a book that carries a smaller, sharper cover.</p>

      <p>The timing lines up with the wider EPUB ecosystem. The EPUB 3.4 revision — whose spec-alignment changes Sigil 2.8.5 also implements (version-specific meta tags, SVG cover href alongside xlink:href, and no longer injecting EPUB 2 cover metadata into EPUB 3 files) — formally opens the door to these formats. On the device side, Kobo's 2026 lineup and PocketBook's newer firmware read AVIF, and E Ink's newer controllers handle it in hardware. Amazon's Kindle ecosystem remains the holdout, so keep a JPEG fallback in your source files if you distribute through KDP.</p>

      <h2>The 2.8.6 emergency: what the cover bug does</h2>

      <p>Within days of 2.8.5 shipping, a critical bug surfaced: if you changed the cover image on an EPUB 3 file in Sigil 2.8.5, the operation could corrupt the book's OPF (the package document that lists every resource). The bug was introduced by the EPUB 3.4 cover-metadata changes and was fixed in 2.8.6, released November 9, 2026.</p>

      <p>A damaged OPF doesn't always announce itself. Symptoms include:</p>

      <ol>
        <li>E-readers or apps refusing to open the file at all ("invalid format" errors)</li>
        <li>Calibre's EPUB check reporting missing or duplicate manifest entries</li>
        <li>The cover silently disappearing on one platform while displaying fine on another</li>
      </ol>

      <p>If you edited any EPUB 3 covers in Sigil 2.8.5 — even one — re-open those files in 2.8.6 (or run them through Calibre's "Check book" tool) to confirm the OPF is intact. Files you only read, or EPUB 2 files, were not affected.</p>

      <h2>Everything else in the 2.8.x pair</h2>

      <p>Beyond the headline items, the two releases accumulate a meaningful quality-of-life batch:</p>

      <ol>
        <li>A redesigned <strong>Adjust Image cropping dialog</strong> (byword77) that is far more usable for cover work</li>
        <li><strong>Multi-selection in the TOC Editor</strong>, so large restructures don't require one entry at a time</li>
        <li>A customizable <strong>Insert Special Characters</strong> dialog driven by a preferences XML file (BeckyEbook)</li>
        <li>Image initial size now shown on Sigil's Image tab</li>
        <li>Plugin wrapper fixes for JavaScript and PLS audio files, plus endnote backlink cleanup in the Aria Clips theme</li>
        <li>Duplicate manifest-entry warnings now grouped into a single load warning instead of dozens of dialogs</li>
        <li>macOS builds remain signed and notarized; the Linux AppImage targets glibc 2.35+ (Ubuntu 22.04 and newer)</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Sigil vs. Calibre after this release</h2>

      <p>The two tools keep converging on features while diverging on purpose. Calibre's editor is improving steadily — the 9.x series added its own accessibility checker and a faster conversion pipeline — but Sigil is where EPUB-specific correctness work happens first, because it's where EPUB-focused users and the spec-conscious maintainers live. If your workflow is "convert, then ship," Calibre is enough. If your workflow is "convert, then make it <em>right</em>," Sigil 2.8.6 is the current baseline. See our <a href="/blog/calibre-vs-sigil-2026/">Calibre vs Sigil comparison</a> for the full decision framework, and the <a href="/blog/epub-conversion-tools-2026/">EPUB conversion tools roundup</a> if you're choosing a pipeline from scratch.</p>

      <h2>How to update safely</h2>

      <p>Download 2.8.6 from the <a href="https://sigil-ebook.com/sigil">official Sigil site</a> — Windows, macOS, and a Linux AppImage are provided. Before upgrading, back up your Sigil preferences folder (location varies by OS; the release notes link the details). After installing, open any EPUB 3 whose cover you changed in 2.8.5 and use File → Validate to confirm the OPF is clean.</p>

      <p>If you work with EPUBs produced by conversion tools — including this site's own EPUB converter — running them through Sigil's validator before sideloading is one of the cheapest quality gates in the whole pipeline.</p>

